**Beautiful code. Head start included.** Ferriki brings the detail of your
editor to docs, blogs, and CMS content. A native Rust engine handles the
highlighting, with familiar Shiki-style HTML calls in Node.js, a Rust API,
and Vite 8 macros for component examples.

## We ship the optimization

A syntax definition tells a highlighter what to match. Ferriki's standard
language packages also carry **compiled regex programs and optimized static
scanner automata**. The optimization becomes part of the download.
Matching stays in Ferroni's native, Oniguruma-compatible engine, with no
translation into JavaScript RegExp. Capture-dependent end and while patterns
remain dynamic to preserve TextMate behavior.

Powered by Ferroni 2.8, we move static regex compilation and Eager scanner
preparation into the asset pipeline. The CDN delivers that prepared work
alongside the grammar. Each new process restores the scanners it needs,
with their static optimizations already in place.

Keep the disk cache between builds and you keep the download, the unpacked
packages, and the preparation. Prepared packages are the default with
prefiltering on. Missing packages fall back to source, and dynamic patterns
remain local. Only requested languages and required dependencies load;
optional code-fence languages stay optional.

**Sponsored by [Sebastian Software](https://sebastian-software.de/)**,
which currently covers Ferriki's CDN costs.

The payoff shows up at both ends: **first HTML takes about half the time of
Source Eager**, while repeated highlighting keeps Eager's execution speed.
In our fourteen-file Blacksmith corpus, Prepared starts within 6% of the
unoptimized Source Off path on both hosts.

| Same 14-file corpus, same HTML as Shiki/WASM | Linux x86-64 | macOS ARM64 |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| First HTML vs. Shiki/WASM | **4.2× faster** | **5.5× faster** |
| Repeated highlighting vs. Shiki/WASM | **3.7× faster** | **4.4× faster** |

Measured October 27, 2026 with Ferriki 1.3.0 and Ferroni 2.8.0. First HTML
includes imports, setup, and rendering in a fresh process with the asset and
decoded caches already populated; downloads, first cache fill, and process
creation are excluded. These are highlighting measurements, not total build
times. [See every mode, machine, and raw result](https://ferriki.dev/evidence/benchmarks#four-regex-policies).

## Choose your path

| If you build with… | Start with… | Guide |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Node.js | `npm install @ferriki/core` | [Node.js package guide](node/ferriki/README.md) |
| Rust | `cargo add ferriki --features remote` | [Rust API guide](docs/rust-api.md) |
| Vite 8 and React | `npm install @ferriki/core @ferriki/vite` | [Vite macros guide](node/vite/README.md) |

## See your first result in Node.js

Ferriki requires Node.js 22.13.0 or newer and uses ESM. npm installs the
matching optional native package for Linux x64/arm64 (glibc or musl), macOS
Apple Silicon, or Windows x64/arm64; keep optional dependencies enabled.

Install the package in your project:

```sh
npm install @ferriki/core
```

Language and theme payloads download from the release-pinned CDN on first use,
are verified with SHA-256, and are cached locally. For offline builds, populate
the cache ahead of time or use a mirror. See [asset loading](docs/asset-loading.md).

Save this complete ESM file as `highlight.mjs`, then run `node highlight.mjs`:

```js
import { writeFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import { codeToHtml } from "@ferriki/core";

const html = await codeToHtml("const answer = 42;", {
  lang: "javascript",
  theme: "github-dark",
});

await writeFile("highlighted.html", html);
console.log("Saved highlighted.html. Open it in a browser to preview your code.");
```

## Reuse one highlighter for repeat work

For a documentation build or CMS worker, create a highlighter with the
languages and themes you need, then keep it around for the blocks you render.
Calls with loaded languages and themes are synchronous. Create one highlighter
per worker, and dispose it when that worker shuts down.

```js
import { createHighlighter } from "@ferriki/core";

const highlighter = await createHighlighter({
  langs: ["typescript", "rust", "css"],
  themes: ["github-light", "github-dark"],
});

try {
  const html = highlighter.codeToHtml("const answer: number = 42;", {
    lang: "typescript",
    theme: "github-dark",
  });
  console.log(html);
} finally {
  highlighter.dispose();
}
```

## What goes over the wire

Prepared scanners add download weight. Here is the tradeoff for the same
requested languages, including their required dependencies:

| Language | Grammar-only CDN payload | Total with prepared scanners |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Rust | 18 kB | 58 kB · 3.2× |
| TypeScript | 0.54 MB | 4.65 MB · 8.6× |
| Markdown | 61 kB | 1.97 MB · 32.2× |

Grammar-only is Ferriki's unoptimized grammar payload; Prepared keeps that
payload and adds compiled programs and automata. Sizes are decimal and
exclude themes and HTTP overhead. Optional fenced-code languages are not
loaded. Keep the cache to reuse these downloads; decoded data takes additional
disk space. [See the exact sizes and source-definition comparison](docs/benchmarks/precompiled-patterns/download-footprint-2026-10-27.md).

Memory looks good in the new capture, too. After 600 documents across twelve
loaded languages, Prepared's whole-process RSS was **45% lower than Shiki/WASM**
on both hosts. For the large TypeScript workload, it was **67–69% lower**.
These are medians from six isolated processes per contender and workload,
with identical HTML. [See the memory measurements](https://ferriki.dev/evidence/benchmarks#process-memory-by-policy).

## Syntax that follows your code

The catalog covers TypeScript, JSX and TSX, Vue, Svelte, Astro, and Angular;
Rust, Python, Go, Java, C/C++, and C#; Markdown, MDX, SQL, JSON, YAML, and TOML;
and build tools from shell scripts and Docker to Terraform and Nix.

TextMate grammars follow the context inside your code: embedded JavaScript
and CSS in HTML, component expressions, tagged templates, and registered
languages in Markdown fences. The catalog contains **67 standard entries for
languages and formats**, plus **27 support grammars** for embeddings and
injections, with **65 themes**. Register custom TextMate grammars for syntax
outside the curated catalog. [Browse the coverage and examples](https://ferriki.dev/guide/languages-and-themes).

## More ways to use Ferriki

| Need | Ferriki provides |
| --- | --- |
| Shiki-shaped HTML | `codeToHtml`, highlighters, singleton helpers, and transformer callbacks for Node.js |
| Stylesheet-driven themes | Nested TextMate scope classes, generated CSS, and theme maps that switch without highlighting again |
| A grammar of your own | Ferriki's curated catalog plus custom TextMate grammars, aliases, and injections; see the [grammar guide](docs/grammars.md) |
| Code in Vite components | `code()` and React `<Code />` macros that prepare highlighted output at build time on Vite 8 |
| Native Rust highlighting | The [`ferriki` crate](https://docs.rs/ferriki): tokens, line HTML, and class-based HTML |

Import `@ferriki/core/macro` or `@ferriki/core/react/macro` in the modules whose
examples you want prepared. The build delivers highlighted output, and React
`<Code />` uses a small framework-free presentation module in the browser.
Grammar payloads stay on the build side; see the [Vite guide](node/vite/README.md)
for the quickstart, SSR behavior, and limits.

## Start with Rust

Enable the remote asset catalog with `cargo add ferriki --features remote`.
This small program highlights a Rust function and prints HTML:

```rust
use ferriki::{
    Highlighter, RemoteAssets, RenderOptions, StandardAssetCatalogs, render_html,
};

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let mut highlighter = Highlighter::builder()
        .with_assets(StandardAssetCatalogs::remote(RemoteAssets::default())?)
        .load_languages(["rust"])
        .load_themes(["github-dark"])
        .build()?;
    let tokens = highlighter.highlight("fn main() {}", "rust", "github-dark")?;
    println!("{}", render_html(&tokens, &RenderOptions::default()));
    Ok(())
}
```

## Compatibility

Ferriki follows semantic versioning: breaking changes to its public API
require a major version; see the [changelog](node/ferriki/CHANGELOG.md). It
pins Shiki v4.4.3 and vscode-textmate v9.3.2 as behavioral references.
Shiki's tests run unchanged from a pinned mirror, each classified as supported,
deferred, or out of scope; the tokenizer is checked against the full
vscode-textmate oracle. Node's public output is HTML, while transformer
callbacks can inspect token and HAST data. The Rust crate exposes tokens and
HTML rendering. See the [compatibility policy](docs/compatibility.md) and
[Shiki migration guide](docs/migrations/shiki-to-ferriki.md).

